### Bias Intensity Evaluation

Evaluates intensity prediction accuracy (confusion matrix + F1) against
`data/debiased_profainty_check_with_keywords.csv` — the only dataset with
real content-level intensity labels (Neutral / Slightly Biased / Highly Biased).

> **Note:** The article holdout set is intentionally NOT used here. Its lean
> labels are source-level (e.g. Fox News → "Right"), so a Fox News article
> about dog rescues would get a "Slightly Biased" derived label, which is
> meaningless as an intensity ground truth.

Default run (500 rows per class, student model):

```bash
python eval_intensity.py
```

All rows:

```bash
python eval_intensity.py --max_per_class 0
```

Different model or custom paths:

```bash
python eval_intensity.py \
  --model_dir "./bias_system_v3/teacher_lean_v3" \
  --encoder bert-base-uncased \
  --out_dir "intensity_eval_outputs"
```

Outputs saved to `intensity_eval_outputs/`:

| File | Contents |
|---|---|
| `cm_intensity_eval.png` | Confusion matrix (counts) |
| `cm_intensity_eval_norm.png` | Normalised confusion matrix |
| `metrics_intensity_eval.json` | Accuracy, macro F1, per-class P/R/F1 |
| `all_metrics.json` | Full metrics dict |

### Evaluate 100-Per-Class Test Set And Store To Database

Runs the model on the balanced 100-per-class CSV and saves each result as an `ArticleAnalysis` row in PostgreSQL (same schema as normal article analysis). Gold label is embedded in the prediction JSON for later DB queries.

```bash
python eval_100_per_class_to_db.py --mode teacher
```

```bash
python eval_100_per_class_to_db.py \
  --csv data_prepared/combined_lean_5class_100_each.csv \
  --mode student \
  --db_url postgresql+psycopg2://user:pass@localhost:5432/thesis
```

DB URL defaults to the `BIAS_DB_URL` environment variable (set in `.env`).

Query per-class accuracy from the database after the run:

```sql
SELECT
    prediction->>'gold_label' AS gold,
    political_bias             AS predicted,
    COUNT(*)                   AS n
FROM article_analyses
WHERE model_config_id = <id>
GROUP BY 1, 2
ORDER BY 1, 2;
```

